# {{crate_name}}

F Prime sequences for this deployment, compiled to WebAssembly and run on board by
[`Svc::WasmSequencer`](https://github.com/nasa/fprime).

## Dependencies

1. Install Rust: https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/getting-started/installation.html

2. Install the Wasm target, `fprime-wasm`, and optionally `wasm-opt` for smaller
   sequences:

```shell
rustup target add {{target}}
cargo binstall fprime-wasm

# optional
cargo binstall wasm-opt
```

## Building

```shell
fprime-wasm build
```

Builds each sequence to `target/{{target}}/release/<sequence>.wasm`, for the
F Prime release named by `FPRIME_VERSION` in `.cargo/config.toml` ({{fprime_version}}).

Uplink the `.wasm`. Keep the `<sequence>.sym` beside it on the ground: it is what
`fprime-wasm backtrace` needs to turn a position the sequence reports back into a
source line, and it only matches the `.wasm` built with it.

## Adding a sequence

```shell
fprime-wasm add <name>
```

- `src/bin/<name>.rs`. The new sequence
- `tests/<name>.rs`. A template for testing this sequence

`fprime-wasm add` also hooks the sequence build into the Cargo (Rust) build system.

## Testing a sequence

```shell
fprime-wasm test
```

Builds the sequences, then runs every test in `tests/`.

## Verifying a sequence

```shell
fprime-wasm verify
```

Loads each sequence as the on-board `WasmSequencer` would, without running it, and
checks it fits the limits in `sequencer.toml`: the ground equivalent of the `LOAD`
command.

### Limits

`sequencer.toml` holds the `WasmSequencer` defaults; change them to match the
deployment. Some are per sequencer instance, so a deployment with several instances
can keep one file per instance, in the project root. A test picks one with:

```rust
#[fprime_test(sequence = "{{sequence}}", limits = "sequencer-payload.toml")]
```

`fprime-wasm verify --limits <path>` sizes against one the same way.

## Debugging a sequence that flew

```shell
fprime-wasm backtrace {{sequence}} --pc <pc>,<pc>,...
```

Names the source lines for the program counters the on-board interpreter reports.
`--offset` takes byte offsets into the `.wasm` instead.

## Dictionary

`{{dictionary}}` is this deployment's dictionary, and `build.rs` turns it into the
typed API the sequences call. Replace it from a newer release build and re-run
`fprime-wasm build`.
